WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.843
Remember the board of bells from the Generics workshop, at the fruit shop's back door?

2
00:00:07.843 --> 00:00:13.345
One bell for each kind of delivery, and everyone who asked to hear a bell came when it rang.

3
00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:16.096
Pedal Post needs the same board, in its depot.

4
00:00:16.346 --> 00:00:21.871
When a rider scans PP-7781 as collected, the load board comes for that bell.

5
00:00:21.871 --> 00:00:24.415
The tracking page wants every bell.

6
00:00:24.415 --> 00:00:28.995
And the handover log, kept for insurance, wants every handover.

7
00:00:29.945 --> 00:00:31.420
Let's set up the words.

8
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.433
The board is the bus, the object in the middle.

9
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:40.523
A handler, a small object that reacts to events, subscribes: it signs up for one type of event.

10
00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:46.356
Code with news publishes an event, and the bus delivers it to every handler that signed up.

11
00:00:46.606 --> 00:00:53.299
Inside, the bus keeps a registry: a map from each event type to the list of handlers that signed up for it.

12
00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:57.051
Each bell is a key, and the names under it are its handlers.

13
00:00:58.001 --> 00:01:00.939
Here are the event types from the last sitting.

14
00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:03.939
A parcel event is booked, failed, or a handover.

15
00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:06.939
And a handover is either collected or delivered.

16
00:01:07.889 --> 00:01:10.716
Now, a question that's easy to be shy about.

17
00:01:10.716 --> 00:01:12.709
A collection is a parcel event.

18
00:01:12.709 --> 00:01:13.801
Everybody agrees.

19
00:01:13.801 --> 00:01:19.393
So if the tracking page signs up for parcel events, why doesn't it hear the collection?

20
00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:25.448
The workshop's board looked up each event by its class when the program runs.

21
00:01:25.448 --> 00:01:33.557
Three handlers sign up: tracking under ParcelEvent, the handover log under Handover, and the load board under ParcelCollected.

22
00:01:33.557 --> 00:01:35.553
Then we publish one collection.

23
00:01:35.553 --> 00:01:36.775
Which handlers run?

24
00:01:39.975 --> 00:01:43.486
The demo prints: exact class only, and then just load.

25
00:01:43.486 --> 00:01:49.013
The event's class is ParcelCollected, so the bus rings that bell, and only that bell.

26
00:01:49.263 --> 00:01:53.127
The tracking page is the most patient listener in the depot.

27
00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:56.606
It waits by a bell that, by design, nobody ever rings.

28
00:01:56.606 --> 00:01:59.053
It compiles, and the page stays blank.

29
00:02:00.003 --> 00:02:00.723
Here's why.

30
00:02:00.723 --> 00:02:04.520
No event's class is ever the interface ParcelEvent itself.

31
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.430
A scan is always one of the leaves: collected, delivered, booked or failed.

32
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.198
The same goes for Handover.

33
00:02:11.198 --> 00:02:16.762
And the last sitting built that middle level on purpose, so the bus has to honour it.

34
00:02:17.712 --> 00:02:19.669
The first honest fix is short.

35
00:02:19.669 --> 00:02:26.780
Keep one flat list of pairs, a type and a handler, and ask each pair: is this event an instance of your type?

36
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.628
It's correct.

37
00:02:27.628 --> 00:02:30.368
But every publish asks every subscription.

38
00:02:30.618 --> 00:02:35.704
Forty handlers means forty questions per scan, and almost all of them are misses.

39
00:02:35.704 --> 00:02:41.355
Picture a porter who walks the whole wall reading every name aloud, for every single scan.

40
00:02:41.355 --> 00:02:42.862
Correct, and exhausting.

41
00:02:43.112 --> 00:02:48.729
What we really want is a board that knows, for each kind of event, exactly which bells to ring.

42
00:02:49.679 --> 00:02:51.106
That's the second fix.

43
00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:53.245
Keep the registry keyed by class.

44
00:02:53.245 --> 00:03:01.414
Then, for each event class, work out the keys to visit: the class itself, then each supertype that is an event, nearest first.

45
00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:06.341
A supertype is a type a class extends or implements, directly or further up.

46
00:03:06.341 --> 00:03:08.870
We'll call that list the dispatch keys.

47
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.319
Let's walk it for a collection.

48
00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:14.933
A queue starts with the event's own class, ParcelCollected.

49
00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:15.668
Take it off.

50
00:03:15.668 --> 00:03:21.671
It's an event, and it's new, so keep it, and put its interface at the back of the queue: Handover.

51
00:03:21.921 --> 00:03:22.853
Take Handover.

52
00:03:22.853 --> 00:03:25.914
Keep it, and queue its interface, ParcelEvent.

53
00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:27.911
Take ParcelEvent, and keep it.

54
00:03:27.911 --> 00:03:31.505
Anything that isn't an event, like Object, is skipped.

55
00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:32.770
The queue is empty.

56
00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:36.570
Three keys: ParcelCollected, Handover, ParcelEvent.

57
00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.936
Publish looks up only those three.

58
00:03:38.936 --> 00:03:40.955
Three bells, not forty names.

59
00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:42.202
Your turn.

60
00:03:42.202 --> 00:03:44.782
What are the keys for DeliveryFailed?

61
00:03:47.982 --> 00:03:50.234
DeliveryFailed, then ParcelEvent.

62
00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:55.078
A failed delivery isn't a handover, so the handover log never hears it.

63
00:03:56.028 --> 00:03:58.190
Now the same demo with the walk.

64
00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:01.298
Walking supertypes: load, handovers, tracking.

65
00:04:01.298 --> 00:04:06.096
The tracking page finally hears the collection, after a very long wait.

66
00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:08.139
And the order is a promise.

67
00:04:08.139 --> 00:04:12.056
The most specific handlers run first, then each wider kind.

68
00:04:12.056 --> 00:04:15.906
Within one key, handlers run in the order they subscribed.

69
00:04:15.906 --> 00:04:23.674
Sign an insurance handler up under Handover, last of all, and the list becomes: load, handovers, insurance, tracking.

70
00:04:24.624 --> 00:04:26.299
Here our picture breaks.

71
00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:32.370
On a real wall, someone would wire the every-parcel bell to ring along with the others.

72
00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.975
Our board has no wires.

73
00:04:33.975 --> 00:04:42.628
The wiring is the class's own family tree, and the bus reads it with plain Java reflection: getInterfaces and getSuperclass.

74
00:04:42.628 --> 00:04:43.815
No extra library.

75
00:04:44.765 --> 00:04:46.659
Here's the heart of the walk.

76
00:04:46.659 --> 00:04:47.965
Take the next class.

77
00:04:47.965 --> 00:04:51.296
Skip it if it isn't an event, or was found already.

78
00:04:51.296 --> 00:04:54.626
Otherwise, queue its interfaces and its superclass.

79
00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:55.998
Kotlin says the same.

80
00:04:58.198 --> 00:05:05.290
The answer never changes for a class, so the bus works it out once per class and caches it, with computeIfAbsent.

81
00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:13.763
On a ConcurrentHashMap that call is one atomic step, so two threads publishing the first collection together can't both fill the cache.

82
00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:17.852
So, subscribe as wide as the job, and no wider.

83
00:05:17.852 --> 00:05:23.928
Tracking under ParcelEvent, the handover log under Handover, the load board under the leaf.

84
00:05:23.928 --> 00:05:30.605
And remember what wide means: a handler for ParcelEvent also hears every event type added next year.

85
00:05:31.555 --> 00:05:34.698
So, why didn't the tracking page hear the collection?

86
00:05:34.698 --> 00:05:38.612
Because the board rang only the bell with the event's exact class.

87
00:05:38.612 --> 00:05:43.949
Now each publish rings its own bell, then each wider one, nearest first, and nothing else.

88
00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:45.029
Quick check.

89
00:05:45.029 --> 00:05:46.759
Publish a DeliveryFailed.

90
00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:50.427
Which of the three run: tracking, handovers, or load?

91
00:05:53.627 --> 00:05:54.573
Only tracking.

92
00:05:54.573 --> 00:06:01.533
The keys are DeliveryFailed and ParcelEvent, and the only handler under those two is the tracking page.
